module engine where

-- structural-dispatch Phase 1 — the predicate-bounded dispatch matcher.
--
-- The NEW dispatch logic (design/structural-dispatch.md, roadmap Phase 1):
-- a typeclass/effect instance registered with a `ByPattern { predicate }`
-- key resolves at dispatch for EVERY type its predicate accepts. After the
-- nominal block -> tag -> global cascade misses, the TS seam in
-- effect-dispatch.ts (resolveEffect) hands this body the structural rules
-- for the class — each `{ predicate, methods }` with `predicate` already
-- lifted to a callable `{$ref: fnId}` value — plus the dispatched type as a
-- marshalled `TraversalType` (the $-key barrier crossing: a `.fenel` body
-- cannot read a raw `$record`/`$variant` object, so the seam marshals it via
-- `marshalTraversalType`, the same boundary adapter the type-algebra bodies
-- use). The DECISION lives here: run each predicate over the type in priority
-- order; the first `True` wins.
--
-- Fenel-first split (roadmap D1-D4 + the Phase-1 brief): the scan + first-match
-- is this body; the TS seam does only enumeration / type-resolution /
-- marshalling / memoisation (no decision logic). The rules arrive
-- priority-sorted (descending) from the seam, so a plain head-to-tail fold IS
-- the priority order — first matching rule wins (the v1 overlap rule; the
-- registration-time disjointness *warning* is deferred, it needs set-algebra
-- subsumption).
--
-- Recursion / purity fence: a predicate is a pure `TraversalType -> Bool`
-- (no `requires`) for Phase 1 — running one must not re-enter structural
-- dispatch. The seam additionally guards the (class, type) key against
-- re-entrant scans; this body assumes purity and does not re-check.

-- matchStructural — fold the priority-ordered structural rules; apply each
-- rule's predicate to that rule's OWN marshalled type `r.ty`; first `True`
-- yields its methods.
-- `rules`'s element is the seam's `{ predicate, methods, ty }` record (predicate
-- is a callable `{$ref}` value; `ty` is the dispatched type marshalled into the
-- VIEW this predicate consumes), kept opaque (`a`) here — we only apply
-- `predicate` to `r.ty` and project `methods`.
-- Phase 5c — each rule carries its OWN marshalled `r.ty` (the per-rule marshal
-- reconciliation, mirroring Phase-2's per-bound `b.ty`): a KIND predicate (over
-- `KindType`) gets the `marshalKindType` view, a STRUCTURAL predicate (over
-- `TraversalType`) the `marshalTraversalType` view — the seam selects per rule
-- from the predicate's declared param type. The shared `ty` param is retained
-- (signature-stable, structural-dispatch-oracle byte-green) but vestigial — the
-- decision reads `r.ty`. For a structural rule `r.ty` IS the same
-- `marshalTraversalType` value the shared `ty` carried, so behaviour is identical.
-- List iteration is via the `head` / `tail` Maybe-returning natives (the
-- engine-body idiom; the array-repr List does not pattern-match Nil / Cons).
-- The predicate is a callable `{$ref}` value carried in a plain field, so it is
-- applied via `$` (low-precedence application) — bare juxtaposition `r.predicate
-- r.ty` would parse `r.predicate` as a call-by-NAME fnId, not a field deref.
matchStructural : (rules: List a) -> (ty: TraversalType) -> Maybe b
matchStructural rules ty =
  case head rules of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just r ->
      if (r.predicate $ r.ty)
        then Just r.methods
        else
          case tail rules of
            Nothing -> Nothing
            Just rest -> matchStructural rest ty

-- isRecordType — the canonical worked example from the design: a structural
-- predicate over the marshalled type. `TtRecord` is the marshalled `$record`
-- shape. A genuinely useful structural recognizer (records as a universe), and
-- the predicate the Phase-1 round-trip test bounds its `ByPattern` rule by.
isRecordType : (t: TraversalType) -> Bool
isRecordType t = case t of
  TtRecord { fields = _ } -> True
  _ -> False

-- A callable's checked arrow type shares one structural representation.
-- Nominal/value representations retain precedence in the scoped resolver.
isArrowType : (t: TraversalType) -> Bool
isArrowType t = case t of
  TtArrow { params = _, returns = _ } -> True
  _ -> False

-- isMapShaped — array-substrate Phase 1a: the structural recognizer for the
-- `$map`/`Map k` former (`TtMap` is the marshalled `$map` shape). This is the
-- predicate the `$map` algebra ByPattern rules (Functor/Foldable/Traversable
-- (Map k), system-entities.ts) bound on: ANY `$map`-shaped type — `Vec n a =
-- $map (Fin n) a`, `Array`, a user `Map String User` — is a member of the
-- algebra through ONE structural instance, no per-type code. Sibling of
-- `isRecordType` (the design's worked structural-predicate template); a pure
-- total `TraversalType -> Bool`, no `requires` (the Phase-1 purity fence).
isMapShaped : (t: TraversalType) -> Bool
isMapShaped t = case t of
  TtMap { values = _ } -> True
  _ -> False

-- structural-dispatch Phase 2 — the monomorphic predicate-BOUND check.
--
-- A function signature can carry a predicate-bound on a type parameter
-- (`f : isRecordType a => a -> b`), riding the SAME `requires`/`=>` constraint
-- machinery supers + effects already use (a bound is "∃ a type satisfying P").
-- At a MONOMORPHIC call site (`f x`, `x`'s type known) the bound is CHECKED by
-- running the predicate over the call-site type — the LIGHT arm (just run a
-- total `TraversalType -> Bool`, no decider). The polymorphic ⊆-subsumption arm
-- (one bound's accepted type-set ⊆ another's) is the deferred, solver-HEAVY 2b.
--
-- This is the SAME operation Phase 1's matcher performs (apply a marshalled-type
-- predicate); Phase 2 reuses the `match-structural.fenel` seam wholesale. The
-- decision logic — iterate the bounds, short-circuit on the first violation,
-- report WHICH bound failed — lives here (Fenel-first); the TS seam only resolves
-- the call-site type, marshals it across the `$`-key barrier, lifts each bound's
-- predicate fn-id to a callable `{$ref}` value, and raises the error.

-- checkPredicateBound — the monomorphic-light CORE: run one predicate over one
-- marshalled call-site type. The predicate is a callable `{$ref}` value carried
-- in a param, so it is applied via `$` (low-precedence application) — bare
-- juxtaposition would parse `predicate` as a call-by-NAME fnId (the Phase-1
-- idiom).
checkPredicateBound : (predicate: a) -> (ty: TraversalType) -> Bool
checkPredicateBound predicate ty = predicate $ ty

-- checkPredicateBounds — fold the call-site's predicate-bounds; each bound is the
-- seam's `{ predicate, ty, label }` record (predicate a `{$ref}` callable, ty the
-- bound param's marshalled concrete type, label the predicate fn-id for the error
-- message). `Nothing` = every bound holds; `Just label` = the FIRST violated
-- bound (short-circuit). Each bound carries its own marshalled `ty` because
-- different bounds may constrain different type-params bound to different concrete
-- types at the call site. List iteration via the `head` / `tail` Maybe-returning
-- natives (the engine-body idiom; the array-repr List does not pattern-match Nil /
-- Cons).
checkPredicateBounds : (bounds: List a) -> Maybe b
checkPredicateBounds bounds =
  case head bounds of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just b ->
      if checkPredicateBound b.predicate b.ty
        then
          case tail bounds of
            Nothing -> Nothing
            Just rest -> checkPredicateBounds rest
        else Just b.label

-- structural-dispatch Phase 4b — the INVERSE view `structuralInstancesFor`.
--
-- matchStructural answers `(class, type) → methods` (ONE class's rules, scanned
-- for the first acceptance). structuralInstancesFor is the INVERSE / the
-- transposition of `resolveStructural`: given a marshalled type + the FULL list
-- of `ByPattern` rules across ALL classes, return the classes whose predicate
-- ACCEPTS the type. This is what the editor's `RepresentationsPanel` surfaces
-- READ-ONLY next to a type's nominal instances — the instances a type gets
-- STRUCTURALLY because its shape matches a derived rule's predicate.
--
-- Each `rules` element is the seam's `{ typeName, predicate, predicateId, ty }`
-- record: `predicate` is the lifted `{$ref}` callable (applied via `$`, the
-- Phase-1 idiom), `predicateId` the predicate fn-id String kept for the
-- provenance label, `ty` the dispatched type marshalled into the VIEW this
-- predicate consumes (Phase 5c — `marshalKindType` for a kind predicate,
-- `marshalTraversalType` for a structural one; selected per rule at the seam).
-- The result element is a PLAIN-keyed `{ typeName, predicate }`
-- record (the body cannot author `$`-prefixed keys; `predicate` here is the
-- fnId String for display, NOT the callable). LIGHT — it RUNS each predicate, no
-- decider (the Phase-1 contract). List iteration is the `head` / `tail` +
-- `appendItem` engine-body idiom. The shared `ty` param is retained
-- (signature-stable) but vestigial — each rule's `r.ty` is the decision input.
structuralInstancesFor : (rules: List a) -> (ty: TraversalType) -> List b
structuralInstancesFor rules ty =
  structuralInstancesForFold rules ty []

-- The head/tail fold behind structuralInstancesFor: walk the marshalled rule
-- list, append `{ typeName, predicate = predicateId }` whenever the rule's
-- predicate accepts the marshalled type. Internal helper.
structuralInstancesForFold : (rules: List a) -> (ty: TraversalType) -> (acc: List b) -> List b
structuralInstancesForFold rules ty acc =
  case head rules of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just r ->
      let acc2 = if (r.predicate $ r.ty)
                   then appendItem { typeName = r.typeName, predicate = r.predicateId } acc
                   else acc
      case tail rules of
        Nothing -> acc2
        Just rest -> structuralInstancesForFold rest ty acc2
